From b11f22a4a09ce84acae82edb0424333c82301c5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DanielBeeck <daniel.beck99@outlook.de>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:17:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] hidpp20: store real macros in the onboard macro memory

Until now the hidpp20 driver only accepted macros that boil down to one
key with modifiers and turned them into a keyboard binding; anything
else was silently lost. Serialize other macros into the device's macro
memory instead: every profile gets the sector right after the profile
sectors, the button bindings point at (sector, offset) and the sector is
written with a CRC on commit.

The reader assumed macro commands never cross a 16 byte boundary and
jumped to the next sector when they did, which made macros written by
Logitech's own software fail with 'error while parsing macro'. Parse a
sector contiguously instead, follow JUMP commands explicitly and know the
size of every command including the one byte flow control commands.

Expose the device-side flow control as new macro events: wait for
release, repeat while pressed and repeat until cancelled (a toggle). The
hidpp20 driver reads and writes them, the other macro capable drivers
reject them with an error, and ratbagd passes them through unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
